'''Nastassja Martin''' (born 1986) is a French anthropologist and essayist. She specialises in the populations of the Far North.

Martin is known for her story ''Croire aux fauves'' (''In the Eye of the Wild)'' in which she describes her attack by a bear.

== Biography ==
Martin was born in [[Grenoble]]. She studied anthropology at the [[School for Advanced Studies in the Social Sciences|École des hautes études en sciences sociales]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Nastassja Martin |url=http://las.ehess.fr/index.php?1161 |access-date=2022-10-18 |website=las.ehess.fr |language=fr}}</ref>

Martin joined the [[Gwichʼin|Gwich'in]],<ref>{{Cite web |last=Calvet |first=Catherine |title=Nastassja Martin : "Je ne présentais pas une menace" |url=https://www.liberation.fr/debats/2019/03/17/nastassja-martin-je-ne-presentais-pas-une-menace_1715732/ |access-date=2022-10-18 |website=Libération |language=fr}}</ref> a hunter-gatherer society, in [[Alaska]], to complete a thesis under the supervision of [[Philippe Descola]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=2019-11-07 |title=" Croire aux fauves ", de Nastassja Martin : l'étreinte de l'ours |language=fr |work=Le Monde.fr |url=https://www.lemonde.fr/critique-litteraire/article/2019/11/07/croire-aux-fauves-de-nastassja-martin-l-etreinte-de-l-ours_6018303_5473203.html |access-date=2022-10-18}}</ref>

In 2016, she published ''Wild Souls'', the story of her experience in Alaska with this population.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Descamps |first=Philippe |date=2016-08-01 |title=Les âmes sauvages. Face à l'Occident, la résistance d'un peuple d'Alaska |url=https://www.monde-diplomatique.fr/2016/08/DESCAMPS/56102 |access-date=2022-10-18 |website=Le Monde diplomatique |language=fr}}</ref>

In August 2015, while she was in the mountains of [[Kamchatka Peninsula|Kamchatka]], on the borders of [[Siberia]], to carry out an anthropological study among the [[Evenks|Evenes]], Nastassja Martin was attacked by a bear. The animal disfigured her, and she lost a piece of her jaw. Months of hospitalization followed in Russia, then in Paris. Based on this experience, she wrote a story, ''Croire aux fauves,'' which was released in October 2019. It recounts her encounter with the bear, her recovery and her animist vision of the world.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2019-11-18 |title=Nastassja Martin: "Il faut repenser le vivant qui est lui-même en train de se repenser" |url=https://www.radiofrance.fr/franceculture/podcasts/par-les-temps-qui-courent/nastassja-martin-il-faut-repenser-le-vivant-qui-est-lui-meme-en-train-de-se-repenser-4455886 |access-date=2022-10-18 |website=France Culture |language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2019-12-17 |title=Nastassja Martin, la femme qui a failli se faire dévorer par un ours |url=https://www.nouvelobs.com/critique/20191217.OBS22430/nastassja-martin-la-femme-qui-a-failli-se-faire-devorer-par-un-ours.html |access-date=2022-10-18 |website=L'Obs |language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-11-30 |title=In the Eye of the Wild by Nastassja Martin review – life after being 'kissed' by a bear |url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2021/nov/30/in-the-eye-of-the-wild-by-nastassja-martin-review-life-after-being-kissed-by-a-bear |access-date=2022-10-18 |website=the Guardian |language=en}}</ref>

In 2020, she took part in a committee against a project to extend the ski area to [[La Grave]] and the [[Massif des Écrins]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=2020-08-02 |title=Le tournant écopolitique de la pensée française |language=fr |work=Le Monde.fr |url=https://www.lemonde.fr/idees/article/2020/08/02/le-tournant-ecopolitique-de-la-pensee-francaise_6047969_3232.html |access-date=2022-10-18}}</ref>

Since then, she has published several other books, such as ''À l'est des rêves,'' which came out in 2022, and which details her anthropological approach.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2022-10-16 |title=Nastassja Martin : " Faire sortir l'anthropologie des cénacles fermés " |language=fr |work=Le Monde.fr |url=https://www.lemonde.fr/livres/article/2022/10/16/nastassja-martin-faire-sortir-l-anthropologie-des-cenacles-fermes_6146013_3260.html |access-date=2022-10-18}}</ref>

